implementationPlan.description:
docs/roles/worker.md, line 13 only: make the landing rule mode-aware. Edit in place and add NO line. Lint caps the file at 40 lines, and it sits in every worker turn's stable system prefix, so stay terse.

Leave line 12 (the qualityGate rule) byte-identical; the verification compares it with HEAD's. HEAD's worktree gate (run_frozen_gate worktree on the merged tree, the land lease) exists only in WT-10's still-WORKING checkpoint d3441bf3. The landed WT-08 path instead refuses every worktree completion while a qualityGate is set. WT-18c (task-cfa129252343438d903de1e67ae6073c) owns that wording; a comment there says so.

Line 13 keeps these verbatim: 'Report EVERY path you created or modified in `complete_step.modifiedFiles`', the every-exit clause, the isolated-clone sentence, the never-revert sentence and the prerequisite sentence. Change only this:
- 'that list is what the wrapper commits' applies to shared mode only.
- 'it commits FROM THE PROJECT ROOT, so work in the project root by default (never a `.worktrees/` checkout)' becomes mode-aware:
  - shared mode keeps 'FROM THE PROJECT ROOT, so work in the project root';
  - worktree mode (`get_context.workspace.mode`): the wrapper has already started you in your task worktree, `.worktrees/<taskId>` on branch `moe/task/<taskId>`, and lands every dirty path there except DENY, so work only there and keep scratch files out of it.
- Drop the outright ban (WT-S comment-ce2c4ee6 on WT-18): any other `.worktrees/` checkout is for a human's explicit request only and is never landed.

Suggested text for the changed span: '— in shared mode that list is what the wrapper commits. It lands a commit on every exit (completion on REVIEW, a `wip(...)` checkpoint otherwise); where you work depends on your task's mode (`get_context.workspace.mode`). In shared mode it commits FROM THE PROJECT ROOT, so work in the project root. In worktree mode the wrapper has already started you in your task worktree, `.worktrees/<taskId>` on branch `moe/task/<taskId>`, and lands every dirty path there except DENY: work only there and keep scratch files out of it. Any other `.worktrees/` checkout is for a human's explicit request only and is never landed (`worker.reference.md`).'

Hard constraints (the verification checks them):
- spell `moe/task/<taskId>` and `.worktrees/<taskId>` exactly so;
- no concrete task id (`task-<32 hex>`) and no 'PREFLIGHT_';
- no phrase matching /never (?:runs|executes|touches) git|runs no git|does not run git/i (daemonGitDocs.test.ts scans this file);
- keep every sentinel: 'what Moe supplied', 'truncated, omitted or missing', 'a navigation aid, never a reason to skip a read', 'once per session', 'advance `contextEpoch` after every compaction', 'isolated-clone delivery', 'moe.record_commit', 'fresh remote fetch', 'sessionId';
- none of LANDING-CONFLICT, LANDING-RETRY, landingRetry, unlanded, land lease, git-land-, refs/moe/board.

Edit with the Edit tool only, never a shell write (GLOBAL rail; mem:gotcha-contested-path-needs-edit-tool-to-land). WT-18 and WT-18c declare this file, so an unwitnessed edit is held at landing.

Verification. Run ONCE, fresh, from the repo root, as ONE command: `<checkRefs[0]> && <checkRefs[1]>`, both exactly as written in the executionContract. Submit that joined command, its exit code and the output tail to complete_task.

- checkRefs[0]:
  - runs npm run lint (the 40-line cap);
  - greps the new content (these greps fail on today's docs);
  - checks that line 12 equals HEAD's, that there is no concrete task id, and that no WT-10..WT-12 vocabulary is present.
- checkRefs[1] takes a `git clone --shared` of this checkout's HEAD with the three files copied in, then:
  - runs parity-check.sh on HEAD's wrappers (epic rail 2; WT-S comment-2f5b6786);
  - runs npm ci and the generator under env -i with nvm node v24.21.0;
  - `cmp`s your initFiles.ts against the fresh output;
  - runs 5 vitest test files: targetedContextGuidance, qaCommitPolicy, initFiles, blockedSeatGuidance and daemonGitDocs.

Planner baseline at HEAD e4e122cd: about 15 s; 5 files and 44 tests passed; parity PASS.

A parity failure on wrapper bytes this task never touched comes from a peer's checkpoint (mem:gotcha-snapshot-gate-peer-wip-checkpoint). Comment on that task with the output, then report_blocked with its id. Never edit a wrapper.

Run everything in the foreground; a one-shot session kills background jobs.
